      "title": "Passage 2: Advances in Precision Agriculture and Sustainable Farming",
      "text": "Paragraph A\nThe development of precision agricultural technology has revolutionized modern crop farming by enabling real-time monitoring of soil chemistry, moisture levels, and crop health. Using autonomous aerial drones equipped with multispectral sensors, farm managers can generate high-resolution spatial maps that pinpoint nutrient deficiencies and pest infestations before visible symptoms manifest on plant foliage. This micro-targeted data allows farmers to apply fertilizers, pesticides, and irrigation water only where needed, substantially reducing input costs and agricultural runoff into freshwater river basins.\n\nParagraph B\nA key component of precision farming is variable rate technology (VRT), which integrates automated machinery with Global Navigation Satellite Systems (GNSS). Seed drills and chemical sprayers fitted with VRT dynamically adjust application rates as tractor machinery moves across fields. Historical field productivity data combined with satellite vegetation indices dictate exact application dosages for each square meter. Soil scientists emphasize that precision application minimizes environmental degradation associated with over-fertilization, such as nitrogen leaching into aquifers and nitrous oxide atmospheric emissions.\n\nParagraph C\nDespite clear economic and environmental advantages, the adoption of precision agriculture faces structural challenges among smallholder farming communities. High initial capital investment for specialized equipment, sensor hardware, and data analytical software presents a significant financial barrier. Furthermore, effective operation requires technical digital literacy and reliable rural broadband connectivity. Agricultural extension services are increasingly developing shared equipment cooperatives and cloud-based mobile decision support platforms to democratize access for mid-sized farming operations.",
