      "title": "Passage 1: Linguistic Anthropology and Indigenous Knowledge Preservation",
      "text": "A\nLinguistic anthropologists have long debated the mechanisms driving language evolution and extinction across isolated human populations. The Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, originally proposed in the early 20th century, posited that the structural grammar and vocabulary of a language fundamentally shape the cognitive perceptions and worldview of its speakers. While extreme forms of linguistic determinism have been largely disproven by modern cognitive psychology, contemporary psycholinguists acknowledge that language exerts subtle, continuous influences on spatial orientation, color categorization, and numerical reasoning.\n\nB\nThe rapid loss of global linguistic diversity represents an irreversible cultural crisis. Anthropologists estimate that of the roughly 7,000 languages spoken worldwide today, over half will become silent by the end of the 21st century. Language endangerment occurs primarily when speaker communities undergo forced assimilation, economic displacement, or cultural hegemony from dominant regional tongues. When an indigenous language ceases to be transmitted to younger generations, unique botanical classifications, oral histories, and ecological knowledge systems accumulated over millennia are permanently lost to humanity.\n\nC\nDigital technologies offer innovative tools for language documentation and revitalization efforts. Computational linguists are constructing interactive open-access digital archives containing phonetic audio recordings, grammatical treatises, and crowdsourced dictionaries for endangered dialects. Furthermore, artificial intelligence language models trained on sparse audiovisual datasets are assisting indigenous educators in developing immersive language-learning mobile applications. Linguists emphasize that while digital archiving preserves structural data, true language vitality ultimately depends on restoring intergenerational daily communication within indigenous communities.\n\nD\nIn traditional indigenous societies, oral language serves as a living repository of ethnobotanical and ecological expertise. Indigenous names for flora and fauna frequently embed detailed observations regarding medicinal properties, seasonal flowering cycles, and ecological interdependencies that modern taxonomies overlook. For instance, Amazonian indigenous dialects classify rainforest medicinal plants based on subtle chemical aromas and micro-habitat preferences, offering pharmacological researchers valuable clues for bioprospecting new therapeutic compounds.\n\nE\nEducational immersion models, such as language nests developed in New Zealand for Maori revitalization and in Hawaii for Native Hawaiian, demonstrate that early childhood immersion produces fluent young speakers. In these programs, elderly native speakers interact exclusively in the ancestral language with young infants and toddlers in preschool settings. By reinstating the natural oral transmission pipeline within community spaces, these initiatives have successfully reversed decades of language decline and revitalized indigenous cultural identity.\n\nF\nInternational legal conventions increasingly recognize linguistic rights as fundamental human rights. The UNESCO International Decade of Indigenous Languages (2022\u20132032) aims to mobilize global resources for language preservation, policy reform, and bilingual education funding. Linguists stress that supporting mother-tongue education not only improves educational attainment among indigenous children but also fosters global cognitive diversity in an increasingly homogenized digital world.",
